now the uk has a new unicorn multiverse  the startup founded by sir tony blair  son ewan has been valued at 1.7 billion  dollars in its latest funding round well  the company was formed in 2016 to train  and find placements for apprentices in  the uk and the united states i'm very  pleased to say you and blair joins me  now you and welcome to you this is only  eight months on from your last funding  rand were you looking to raise more  capital  we were looking to raise more capital  but there was a huge amount of interest  in what we were doing  and as market conditions have shown at  the moment it's never a bad thing to be  able to have more capital and to be able  to invest in the programs you want to  invest in  very interesting point you make that i  mean does this in any way then reflect  unease uh on your part at the sort of  outlook for fundraising later this year  no i i think look it's it's very clear  that the the fundraising outlook is is  not necessarily smooth i think the thing  that's been important for us is if you  look at priorities for businesses and  and i speak to a lot of ceos and leaders  of businesses  diversity and digital transformation are  two of the biggest things they focus on  and the best way of addressing those  issues in tandem is through an  apprenticeship program so it's created  a renewed interest in apprenticeships  and a big focus on what we're doing  and how are you going to be putting this  capital to work  so we're going to be investing a lot  more in the depth of our programs in  ensuring for example in data analytics  you think about can become specialists  in data science in allowing someone  who's doing software engineering to be  able to pursue that over several years  of their career we're going to be  investing a lot more in our community in  the social aspects of apprenticeships  the professional networking aspects  we're going to be building out our tech  platform and our product  i mean you're obviously very passionate  about apprenticeships do you think  you're winning the argument though that  they're a valid alternative to going to  university  i think so and i look our success is  actually in many ways reflective of that  i mean we've grown  nine times over the last two years we we  launched the us last year we're now in  nine different cities we've launched  programs with google verizon cisco visa  and many others we work with over 500  employers across government tech  professional services engineering and  healthcare and that is is as much as  anything an endorsement of the fact that  more and more companies are seeing  apprenticeships is not just the best way  to to hire and attract diverse talent  but actually to develop their people as  they need to shift rapidly to to  focusing on digital data and tech as  high growth skills areas  but what about the would-be consumers of  those apprenticeships i mean if i look  at university applications last year it  was three quarters of a million i think  only about 200 000 people started  apprenticeships in this country  that's correct though the fastest  growing area is in tech and professional  services and if you think about it for  an individual  it's such a compelling proposition you  get the job you want the skills you need  one-on-one coaching you're running a  salary from day one you're not  accumulating any debt and with  multiverse you still get these these  social and networking experiences  through our community and we're seeing  an inundation of people applying for our  roles um in fact in the us we have  probably about 50 applications for every  one role and we've had 200 000  applications over the last year or so  which are the companies that you'd most  like to get on board that you haven't uh  had participating to date  i probably won't call out specific  companies but really anyone who is a big  employer in tech or professional  services we want to be working with and  actually nearly every company in the  world now because of the the shifts that  are happening in their business and  their workforce needs  has growing needs in data in digital and  tech and so it is incumbent on them to  look at using apprenticeships to create  those opportunities but also attract the  skill talent they need  and what about the valuation that you've  achieved in this latest funding round i  mean by any conventional investment  yardstick multiverse is now valued at a  colossal multiple of its sales does that  make you at all uncomfortable  no it doesn't as i said i think if  anything it's an endorsement of the fact  that people look at the world at the  moment and realize that a  one-size-fits-all model around  university or college is completely  insufficient and actually really unfair  and we've all got to make sure that the  best jobs of the next decade don't  simply go to all the same people as the  best jobs the last decade otherwise  we're building up big social problems  we're going to impact on productivity  even more and we're not going to end up  with a fair or equal society so it  doesn't benefit employers it doesn't  benefit individuals it doesn't benefit  society more widely and people have seen  in what we're doing at multiverse a  chance to make this a centerpiece of  that new movement around building an  alternative to university through  apprenticeships  who do you think you're competing with  is there much of a competitive landscape  out there  there are lots of other organizations  delivering apprenticeships many on  a much smaller scale i think i often  look at our biggest competition as  traditional colleges and universities  because for a long time they've been  able to act as the gatekeepers of a lot  of the best jobs in the labor market  with very little pushback or challenge  despite the fact there's no real  correlation between academics and job  performance so i i think for us it's  often about showing actually how this  route can be  an even better way to help people  transition fairly from full-time  education into full-time employment and  then crucially continue to develop them  because people are going to be in the in  the workforce now for at least 50 years  everyone's going to have to train and  retrain multiple times and a shot of  learning at the start of your career  through an academic institution is just  completely insufficient for what's going  to be needed and how about you  personally obviously there are all sorts  of reports this morning about the value  of your stake in the business following  this latest funding round i mean  i guess you've been in the public eye  all your life really to an extent does  it feel like there's added pressure on  you personally  i i don't think so and look it's  something she said i've dealt with a lot  of that throughout my life it's not  something i pay a huge amount of  attention to the thing i always  emphasize is the money hasn't gone to me  it's gone to multiverse and we have an  incredible team of people and leaders  who are making what we're doing happen  on a daily basis so it is about much  more than me  and i think you've got 600 employees  right now where do you think that will  be this time next year  probably come close to doubling that  we're hiring in just about every area  particularly into our tech and product  teams but also we're hiring coaches  we're hiring people into our community  team we're hiring people to work with  employers on implementing and embedding  programs so  we're continuing to grow  all right you and bled good to talk to  you this morning thanks very much indeed  thanks thank you
 

